The Epistemic Failure Atlas

A Formal Framework for the Cartography of Claim Mortality in Scientific Knowledge

---

Abstract

This dissertation develops a theoretical framework for the systematic representation, comparison, and predictive modelling of the death of scientific claims. It departs from the commonplace genre of the "history of errors" and proposes instead a formal ontology in which every scientific proposition is decomposed into an atomic claim C equipped with an explicit validity domain D(C), a protective belt of auxiliary hypotheses A(C), a signed evidential body E^\pm(C), a set of rival formulations \mathcal{R}(C), and a set of falsification conditions \mathcal{F}(C). The epistemic state of C is represented not as a discrete verdict but as a time-indexed probability vector \vec{S}_t(C) over eight terminal and non-terminal categories (supported, uncertain, restricted, superseded, abandoned, refuted, open, internally inconsistent), whose trajectory encodes the historical career of the claim. We introduce the Epistemic Failure Card (EFC) as the unit record of this representation, and formulate a triadic adversarial protocol G \rightleftharpoons K \rightarrow A — Generator, Kritik, Arbiter — grounded in a citation-verified corpus layer that forbids the circulation of unsourced assertions. The framework is meta-epistemically reflexive: the Atlas itself is submitted to its own failure card, and its abandonment conditions are explicitly stated. Twelve canonical historical trajectories (aether, phlogiston, miasma, Ptolemaic geocentrism, spontaneous generation, Lamarckian soft inheritance, classical blackbody radiation, Galenic humoralism, Gai Tian hemispherical cosmology, Marxian labor-value theory, Béchamp's pleomorphism, Cuvierian catastrophism) are reinterpreted under the framework. No experimental or observational work is performed: the method is philosophical, formal, and historical. The dissertation concludes with an analysis of the risk of industrialized epistemic failure in the age of large language models, and argues that a civilization capable of generating thousands of theories per day requires — as a condition of its own rationality — a symmetric capacity to kill them quickly and verifiably.

Keywords: philosophy of science, epistemology, formal ontology, falsification, Duhem–Quine thesis, Lakatos, Bayesian epistemology, claim mortality, epistemic failure, large language models, meta-science.

