Chapter 2 — Historical and Philosophical Foundations

2.1 Popper: falsifiability as a criterion

Popper's central move was to relocate the demarcation between science and non-science from the content of a claim to its logical form. A claim is scientific iff it forbids something. Formally:

C \text{ is scientific} \iff \exists O : \neg O \text{ is conceivable and } C \implies O.

Falsification, on Popper's account, is asymmetric: no amount of confirming evidence can prove C, but one counterinstance can refute it.

The framework of this dissertation accepts the demarcation criterion but rejects the asymmetry in its strong form. Popper's picture ignores the Duhem–Quine problem (no claim is tested in isolation) and the Lakatosian observation that a single anomaly rarely kills a live research programme. We therefore adopt:

C \text{ is scientific} \iff \mathcal{F}(C) \neq \emptyset,

where \mathcal{F}(C) is the set of falsification conditions. The claim must have falsification conditions; whether any single condition, once met, suffices to kill it, is a separate question.

2.2 Kuhn: paradigms and incommensurability

Kuhn's contribution is the observation that science does not advance by piecemeal refutation but by wholesale paradigm replacement, and that the choice between paradigms is not fully determined by evidence. The famous "incommensurability" thesis asserts that rival paradigms may not share a common evidential vocabulary, so that translation between them is partial.

For our purposes, Kuhn matters because he identifies a class of claim deaths that are not refutations. A Ptolemaic astronomer's claim that "Venus does not exhibit a full phase cycle" is not refuted by Galileo's observation; it is reinterpreted within a new framework in which "Venus" and "phase" mean different things. The EFC formalism must accommodate this: the formulation of a claim may be revised (Chapter 4's C_0 \rightarrow C_1 \rightarrow \dots trajectory) without the claim identity being destroyed.

2.3 Lakatos: research programmes and protective belts

Lakatos refines Kuhn by distinguishing the hard core of a research programme from its protective belt. The hard core is treated as irrefutable by methodological decision; the belt is where auxiliary hypotheses live and die. A programme is progressive if it generates novel predictions, degenerating if its auxiliary moves only accommodate existing anomalies post hoc.

Lakatos's framework is the closest ancestor of the present one. Our A(C) is his protective belt. Our distinction between auxiliary hypotheses with high and low vulnerability scores is a quantitative refinement of his qualitative analysis. Our \vec{S}_t(C) trajectory is designed to make explicit whether a claim's historical career has been one of progressive extension or degenerate rescue.

We depart from Lakatos in one respect: we do not privilege research programmes over claims. The claim, not the programme, is our atomic unit, because the programme is analysable as a graph of claims (Chapter 4).

2.4 Duhem–Quine and the underdetermination of theory by evidence

The Duhem–Quine thesis, in its strong form, asserts that any claim can be preserved in the face of any evidence by suitable adjustment of auxiliary hypotheses. In its weak form, it asserts that the distribution of refutation cost across a claim-plus-belt conjunction is not determined by the evidence alone.

The strong form is almost certainly false: there are claims (e.g., "the mass of the electron is exactly 9.1 \times 10^{-31} kg to twenty decimal places") for which no plausible auxiliary adjustment can save them from ordinary measurement. The weak form is almost certainly true and is a foundational constraint on any theory of refutation.

We adopt the weak form and formalize it as follows. When an anomaly \neg O is observed against C \land A(C), the posterior distribution over which conjunct is at fault is given by Bayes' rule applied to the space \{C, a_1, \dots, a_n\}. The prior P(C) is not given by the evidence; it is a prior in the technical sense.

2.5 Bayesian epistemology

Bayesian confirmation theory provides the natural formalism for belief updating. Given evidence e, the posterior probability of a claim C is

P(C \mid e) = \frac{P(e \mid C) P(C)}{P(e)}.

This is elegant but underdetermined: both P(C) (the prior) and P(e \mid C) (the likelihood) are themselves not given by the evidence. In practice they are supplied by background knowledge, by analogy, or by stipulation.

We adopt Bayesianism as the natural semantics of the status vector, but we do not treat the prior as epistemically innocent. The prior is part of what a community brings to the table; the EFC formalism requires that priors and their sources be made explicit.

2.6 Synthesis: a hybrid framework

The framework developed in the following chapters is a hybrid. It takes from Popper the falsification criterion (\mathcal{F}(C) \neq \emptyset), from Kuhn the recognition of non-refutational death, from Lakatos the protective-belt structure and the distinction between progressive and degenerating programmes, from Duhem–Quine the underdetermination of fault distribution, and from Bayesianism the probabilistic semantics of belief states.

No single received framework subsumes the others. The contribution of this dissertation is to build one that does.

