Chapter 3 — Ontology of the Claim

3.1 What is a claim?

We define a claim as a declarative sentence \psi together with a specification of the conditions under which \psi is asserted to hold. Formally, a claim is a triple

C = \langle \psi, D(C), \sigma \rangle,

where:

· \psi is the formulation of the claim;
· D(C) is the validity domain — the set of conditions under which \psi is asserted;
· \sigma is the assertion type: an element of a small alphabet \Sigma, e.g.,

\Sigma = \{\text{universal, statistical, causal, existential, structural, normative}\}.

The assertion type matters because falsification conditions differ by type. A universal claim is falsified by a single counterinstance; a statistical claim is falsified by distributional discrepancy; a causal claim requires intervention or a defensible identification strategy; a structural claim is falsified by formal inconsistency or by rival structures with superior fit.

3.2 The claim–theory distinction

A theory is a set of claims closed under some inferential relation:

T = \{C_1, C_2, \dots, C_m\}.

To speak of a theory as "refuted" is shorthand. Strictly, individual claims are refuted, restricted, superseded, or abandoned; a theory's status is a functional of the statuses of its constituent claims:

S(T) = \Phi(S(C_1), \dots, S(C_m)).

The functional \Phi is not always simple. A theory may retain value even when several of its central claims are refuted, if the surviving claims continue to do explanatory work. Newtonian mechanics after relativity is not "false"; it is a restricted fragment of a larger structure.

3.3 The claim identity problem

When a claim is reformulated — say, when Einstein restates the aether claim as a claim about the geometric structure of spacetime — is it the same claim? Or is it a different claim that happens to occupy a similar functional role?

We adopt the persistence through reformulation principle: a claim is individuated by a canonical identifier that survives revisions to its formulation, provided the revisions preserve (a) the phenomenon the claim is invoked to explain, and (b) the inferential role of the claim within its containing theory. When either condition fails, the reformulated claim is a new claim, and the old claim's trajectory is terminated.

Formally, we represent this via a versioned identity:

\text{Claim ID}: \text{CLAIM-DOMAIN-NNNN}, \quad \text{Version}: v \in \mathbb{N}.

The claim at version v is a distinct ClaimVersion node in the knowledge graph; but the chain

C^{(0)} \rightarrow C^{(1)} \rightarrow C^{(2)} \rightarrow \dots

is the trajectory of a single claim-identity.

3.4 Atomicity

We require claims to be atomic in the following sense: a claim \psi is atomic if it can be falsified by a single observation type. Composite claims of the form \psi_1 \land \psi_2 must be decomposed into their conjuncts before entering the atlas.

This requirement is not merely methodological; it is a response to a well-known pathology of scientific discourse. Compound claims allow their defenders to shelter behind the stronger conjunct when the weaker is attacked, and vice versa. Decomposition exposes the vulnerability of each component.

3.5 Domains of validity

The validity domain D(C) is a subset of the parameter space relevant to \psi. For a physical claim, D(C) may be a region in (v, \Phi, T, \dots)-space. For a biological claim, it may be restricted by taxonomic scope, by developmental stage, or by environmental conditions. For an economic claim, it may be restricted by market structure, institutional context, or time horizon.

We write:

D(C) \subseteq \mathcal{P},

where \mathcal{P} is the parameter space associated with the domain of C.

A claim's status is domain-relative. The statement "Newtonian mechanics is false" is not well-formed. The statement "Newtonian mechanics is false outside D(C) = \{(v, \Phi) : v \ll c, \Phi \ll c^2\}" is well-formed — and is close to the modern consensus.

3.6 Assertion types and their falsification profiles

Different assertion types have different falsification signatures. We list them here as a reference table, to be used in Chapter 5 when specifying \mathcal{F}(C).

Type Form Falsification condition
Universal \forall x \in D, P(x) One x_0 \in D with \neg P(x_0).
Statistical P(X \in A) = p Sample frequency deviating beyond a specified tolerance under adequate power.
Causal X \rightarrow Y Intervention on X fails to alter Y, or a confounder accounts for the association.
Existential \exists x : P(x) Exhaustive search over D failing to find x.
Structural \mathcal{S} is the structure of P Alternative structure \mathcal{S}' with strictly better fit and no additional parameters.
Normative X ought to be Y Internal inconsistency of the normative framework, or failure to satisfy its own stated criteria.

This table is not exhaustive. It is a starting point, to be extended as the atlas grows.

