Part IV — Historical Reinterpretation

Chapter 10 — Twelve Canonical Trajectories

10.1 Method

The twelve cases below are drawn from physics, chemistry, biology, medicine, geology, astronomy, and economics. Each is presented in a standard format: formulation, domain, auxiliary belt, trajectory, killer proof, status vector at present, and lesson.

This is a library test: no new experiment or observation is performed. The framework is applied to existing historical material, and its descriptive adequacy is assessed by whether it captures the essential features of each trajectory. The twelve cases are not a sample in any statistical sense; they are chosen for their diversity and their representativeness of major claim-death types.

10.2 Case 1 — The luminiferous aether

Formulation. Light waves require a continuous mechanical medium (the luminiferous aether) for their propagation.

Domain. Classical electrodynamics and optics, v \ll c, \Phi \ll c^2.

Belt. Aether is stationary in absolute space; partial aether drag; Lorentz–FitzGerald contraction as an auxiliary rescue.

Trajectory. 1880: supported (0.90). 1887: uncertain (0.40/0.50). 1905: superseded (0.95).

Killer proof. Does the speed of light vary with the observer's motion relative to an absolute frame?

Status (2024). \vec{S} = (0, 0, 0, 0.95, 0.05, 0, 0, 0). Superseded.

Lesson. A claim with a large, flexible belt can survive decisive refutation for years. It dies not by refutation but by supersession: the demonstration that the belt is not needed.

10.3 Case 2 — The phlogiston theory of combustion

Formulation. Combustion is the release of a fire-like substance (phlogiston) from combustible bodies.

Domain. 18th-century chemistry.

Belt. Phlogiston can possess negative weight; air is a passive absorbent.

Trajectory. 1700: supported. 1780: uncertain. 1789: refuted.

Killer proof. Does metallic calcination increase or decrease the mass of the isolated system?

Status (2024). \vec{S} = (0, 0, 0, 0.10, 0, 0.85, 0, 0.05). Refuted.

Lesson. The phlogiston case shows a claim dying by internal contradiction with a well-established auxiliary (mass conservation), not by a single decisive experiment. The death was distributed across Rey, Black, Scheele, and Lavoisier over more than a century.

10.4 Case 3 — Miasma theory of cholera

Formulation. Epidemic cholera is transmitted by inhalation of toxic atmospheric emanations from decaying matter.

Domain. 19th-century epidemiology.

Belt. Higher elevation protects from miasmas; foul odour correlates with virulence.

Trajectory. 1830: supported. 1854: contested. 1880: refuted and superseded.

Killer proof. Does cholera incidence correlate with water source independent of elevation and odour?

Status (2024). \vec{S} = (0, 0, 0, 0.05, 0, 0.95, 0, 0). Refuted.

Lesson. Refuted and superseded: miasma theory was refuted as a complete account of cholera, and superseded as a general theory of disease by germ theory. The two status categories are not mutually exclusive in practice.

10.5 Case 4 — Ptolemaic geocentrism

Formulation. Earth is stationary at the centre of the universe; all celestial bodies move on epicycles and deferents.

Domain. Classical astronomy.

Belt. Equant points; fifth element (aether) for celestial bodies.

Trajectory. 150 AD: supported. 1543: contested. 1610: weakened by Galileo's observations. 1687: superseded by Newtonian mechanics. 1838: fully superseded by parallax measurement.

Killer proof. Does Venus exhibit a complete phase cycle consistent with a sun-centred orbit?

Status (2024). \vec{S} = (0, 0, 0, 0.99, 0.01, 0, 0, 0). Superseded.

Lesson. A claim can be superseded over centuries, through a cascade of independent events, no single one of which is decisive. The framework must accommodate slow deaths.

10.6 Case 5 — Spontaneous generation

Formulation. Microscopic living organisms generate de novo from non-living matter.

Domain. 19th-century microbiology.

Belt. Air supplies a vital force; boiling alters the broth's capacity to generate life.

Trajectory. Antiquity: supported. 1668 (Redi): contested. 1861 (Pasteur): refuted.

Killer proof. Do boiled infusions in swan-neck flasks exposed to unfiltered air remain sterile?

Status (2024). \vec{S} = (0, 0, 0, 0.01, 0, 0.99, 0, 0). Refuted.

Lesson. The Pasteur–Pouchet debate shows the role of experimental design in claim death. The same phenomenon (microbial growth in broth) supported both positions until the swan-neck flask isolated the causal variable.

10.7 Case 6 — Lamarckian soft inheritance

Formulation. Acquired somatic modifications during an organism's lifetime alter its heritable germline traits.

Domain. 19th-century evolutionary theory.

Belt. Gemmules or pangenes carry somatic information to germ cells; the germline is not shielded.

Trajectory. 1809: supported. 1893 (Weismann): weakened. 1930s: superseded. 1948–1964 (Lysenko): politically sustained in the USSR against the evidence.

Killer proof. Does somatic modification directly rewrite germline genomic sequences?

Status (2024). \vec{S} = (0, 0.10, 0.20, 0.65, 0, 0.05, 0, 0). Superseded, with a small residual restricted domain (epigenetic inheritance).

Lesson. The Lysenko episode shows the coupling between epistemic status and institutional support. A refuted claim can persist for decades if sustained by political authority. The atlas must represent institutional variables explicitly.

10.8 Case 7 — Classical blackbody radiation

Formulation. Thermal radiation in a cavity scales continuously with frequency squared, without bound.

Domain. Late 19th-century electrodynamics.

Belt. Energy equipartition holds for all modes; oscillator-field coupling is continuous.

Trajectory. 1900: internally inconsistent (Rayleigh–Jeans divergence). 1900 (Planck): superseded.

Killer proof. Does the total integrated radiative energy density diverge?

Status (2024). \vec{S} = (0, 0, 0, 0.10, 0, 0.10, 0, 0.80). Internally inconsistent, then superseded.

Lesson. Internal inconsistency is a distinct death type. The ultraviolet catastrophe is not a refutation by observation but a contradiction between the claim and the mathematics of the framework it belongs to. The status vector's eighth category is essential here.

10.9 Case 8 — Galenic humoralism

Formulation. Health is the balance of four humours (blood, phlegm, yellow bile, black bile); disease is dyscrasia.

Domain. Ancient and medieval medicine.

Belt. Phlebotomy restores eucrasia; each humour maps to elemental qualities.

Trajectory. 175 AD: supported. 1628 (Harvey): contested. 1858 (Virchow): superseded.

Killer proof. Can disease aetiologies be isolated histologically and microbiologically without reference to humoral dyscrasia?

Status (2024). \vec{S} = (0, 0, 0, 0.10, 0.10, 0.80, 0, 0). Refuted and superseded.

Lesson. A claim can dominate a field for 1700 years. The atlas must be able to represent long persistence times and to distinguish between the medical claim (refuted) and the practical recommendations that may have had independent value (e.g., some dietary advice).

10.10 Case 9 — Gai Tian hemispherical cosmology

Formulation. The heavens form a hemispherical rotating dome over a square planar Earth; solar seasonal variation arises from changing radial distance along seven concentric paths.

Domain. Ancient Chinese astronomy.

Belt. Sunset is visual extinction of the sun at the horizon, not its physical descent.

Trajectory. ~100 BC: supported. 100 AD (Huntian): contested. 200 AD: superseded by Huntian spherical model.

Killer proof. Do latitude-dependent gnomon shadow ratios fit planar or spherical geometry?

Status (2024). \vec{S} = (0, 0, 0, 0.85, 0.15, 0, 0, 0). Superseded.

Lesson. The Gai Tian case is a reminder that non-Western cosmologies are not folk beliefs but quantitative models that made predictions and were superseded by better models. The atlas's neutrality about cultural origin is essential.

10.11 Case 10 — Marxian labour theory of value

Formulation. The exchange-value of a reproducible commodity is objectively determined by the socially necessary labour time required for its production.

Domain. Political economy, 19\text{th}–21\text{st} centuries.

Belt. The transformation problem; the role of marginal utility as short-term noise around a labour-value axis.

Trajectory. 1867: supported. 1896 (Böhm-Bawerk): contested. 1960 (Sraffa): reinterpreted. 2024: contested and open.

Killer proof. Can equilibrium prices in sectors of differing capital intensity be derived deterministically from labour inputs without recourse to subjective time preference?

Status (2024). \vec{S} = (0.05, 0.30, 0.20, 0.20, 0, 0.05, 0.20, 0). Uncertain and open.

Lesson. Not every claim dies. Some remain contested indefinitely, and the atlas must represent this without forcing a verdict. The Marxian case is also a warning: the status vector can be ideologically distorted. The Arbiter A must be calibrated against this risk.

10.12 Case 11 — Béchamp's pleomorphism and terrain

Formulation. Infectious disease originates from internal physiological imbalance causing host microzymas to mutate into pathogenic forms.

Domain. 19th-century microbiology.

Belt. Aseptic tissues autonomously generate microbes at pH/redox thresholds; bacterial species are transient stages of universal cellular forms.

Trajectory. 1883: supported by a minority. 1885–1900: refuted and superseded by germ theory.

Killer proof. Does inoculating a healthy host with a pure-culture pathogen strain induce specific disease symptoms independently of prior terrain dysregulation?

Status (2024). \vec{S} = (0, 0, 0.05, 0, 0.10, 0.85, 0, 0). Refuted and abandoned.

Lesson. Béchamp was substantially wrong. But his broader intuition — that host state matters — has partially returned in modern microbiome and immunology research. The atlas should distinguish the refuted claim (pleomorphism) from the partially rehabilitated intuition (host terrain). This distinction is precisely what the restricted and open categories are for.

10.13 Case 12 — Cuvierian catastrophism

Formulation. Earth's stratigraphic discontinuities and mass faunal replacements require episodic paroxysmal events that present-day observable rates cannot account for.

Domain. 19th-century geology and palaeontology.

Belt. Present-day causes are insufficient for mountain building and marine transgressions; violent crustal collapses recur.

Trajectory. 1812: supported. 1830 (Lyell): contested. 1980 (Alvarez): partially rehabilitated via impact events.

Killer proof. Can sedimentary rock formations and fossil successions be fully accounted for by slow uniform accumulation without invoking episodic high-energy events?

Status (2024). \vec{S} = (0.10, 0.10, 0.55, 0.20, 0, 0.05, 0, 0). Restricted; partially rehabilitated.

Lesson. Cuvier's catastrophism is not simply dead. The discovery of the Chicxulub impact and of the Siberian Traps gave a partial rehabilitation to the structural claim (that some geological transitions require episodic high-energy events) while leaving the ontological claim (that all transitions are catastrophic) refuted. The atlas must handle partial rehabilitation without conflating it with original validity.

