Part V — Consequences

Chapter 13 — The Industrialization of Error in the Age of Large Language Models

13.1 The coherence–truth gap

A large language model produces text that is often coherent, well-formed, stylistically appropriate, and plausible. The property of coherence is not, however, equivalent to the property of truth:

\text{coherence} \not\Rightarrow \text{truth}.

The gap has always been present in human reasoning; LLMs make it industrially visible. A model can produce a hundred pages of internally consistent argumentation for a false premise without ever signalling uncertainty.

13.2 The generator asymmetry

Prior to the LLM era, the cost of generating a theory was roughly comparable to the cost of destroying one. Both required human cognitive effort; both were constrained by the availability of the researcher's time.

The LLM changes the ratio. Let c_G be the cost of generating a theory and c_K the cost of refuting one. In the human regime,

\frac{c_G}{c_K} \approx 1.

In the LLM regime,

\frac{c_G}{c_K} \to 0.

Generation is nearly free; destruction still requires verification against an external corpus, which is bounded by human labour and by the finite size of the corpus itself.

This asymmetry is a structural epistemic risk. A civilization that can generate thousands of theories per day and can refute only a few will accumulate an ever-growing backlog of unresolved claims. If the backlog is not managed, the effective epistemic state of the civilization degrades, even as its generative capacity expands.

13.3 The response: industrialized destruction

The natural response is not to slow generation but to industrialize destruction. The adversarial protocol G \rightleftharpoons K \rightarrow A is a design for exactly this. It scales refutation to match generation by exploiting the same computational substrate: LLMs generate, LLMs attack, LLMs arbitrate — but with different models, different prompts, and different evidentiary obligations.

The Grounding Layer is the critical constraint. Without it, industrial-scale attack becomes industrial-scale hallucination. With it, attacks are anchored in the finite, verifiable corpus of primary sources.

13.4 The corpus bottleneck

The finite corpus is the ultimate limit. No adversarial protocol can refute a claim whose refutation requires evidence not yet collected. The atlas's capacity to kill claims is bounded by the corpus, and the corpus is bounded by the practical rate of scientific production.

The implication is that not all claims can be killed in real time. Some must be left in an open state, with their belt intact, awaiting future evidence. The atlas must represent this honestly: open is not a failure of the atlas, but a description of the world.

13.5 A structural argument for epistemic hygiene

The argument of this chapter can be summarized as follows.

1. LLMs radically reduce the cost of generating theories.
2. A civilization that generates faster than it can refute accumulates unresolved claims.
3. Unresolved claims accumulate as a form of epistemic debt.
4. The debt is discharged only by refutation, restriction, or supersession.
5. Therefore, a civilization that wishes to preserve its epistemic integrity must develop refutation capacity proportional to its generation capacity.

The Atlas is one instrument for this. It is not the only one; it is not sufficient on its own. But it is an instrument of the right shape.

