Appendix F — Status Vector Dynamics: Formal Derivations

F.1 Bayesian Update on the Simplex

Given a status vector \vec{S}_{t^-}(C) and an event e with likelihood vector \vec{\lambda}_e, the posterior is

\vec{S}_{t^+}(C) = \frac{\vec{S}_{t^-}(C) \odot \vec{\lambda}_e}{\langle \vec{S}_{t^-}(C), \vec{\lambda}_e \rangle},

where \langle \cdot, \cdot \rangle is the Euclidean inner product.

The likelihood vector is not given by the event alone. It depends on the belt state A_t(C):

\vec{\lambda}_e = \vec{\lambda}(e, A_t(C)).

The dependency on A_t(C) captures the fact that the same event has different evidential force depending on which auxiliary hypotheses are in place.

F.2 The Duhem-Quine Fault Distribution

When an anomaly \neg O is observed against C \land a_1 \land \dots \land a_n, the fault distribution over the conjuncts is given by

P(a_i \text{ at fault} \mid \neg O) = \frac{P(\neg O \mid a_i \text{ at fault}) P(a_i \text{ at fault})}{\sum_{j} P(\neg O \mid a_j \text{ at fault}) P(a_j \text{ at fault})}.

The prior P(a_i \text{ at fault}) is not given by the evidence. It is provided by the vulnerability scores:

P(a_i \text{ at fault}) \propto v(a_i).

This makes explicit the fact that the vulnerability scores encode the community's prior over which auxiliary is likely to be blamed under evidential pressure.

F.3 Rival Coupling

The trajectory of a claim C is coupled to the trajectories of its rivals. Let \mathcal{R}(C) = \{C'_1, \dots, C'_m\}. The joint dynamic is

\vec{S}_{t^+}(C) = f\left(\vec{S}_{t^-}(C), \{\vec{S}_{t^-}(C'_j)\}_{j=1}^m, e\right).

In the special case where the rivals are mutually exclusive and exhaustive, f reduces to the ordinary Bayesian update with a normalization over the union of categories. In general, f must account for the fact that rival frameworks may not share a common category set.

We propose the following general form:

f = \vec{S}_{t^-}(C) \odot \left( \vec{\lambda}_e + \sum_{j=1}^m \mu_{C, C'_j} \cdot \vec{\phi}_{C'_j} \right),

where \mu_{C, C'_j} \in [0,1] is the rivalry strength between C and C'_j, and \vec{\phi}_{C'_j} is a vector encoding the competitive pressure that C'_j exerts on C. When C'_j is winning, \vec{\phi}_{C'_j} has mass on the superseded and refuted categories for C.

The form is heuristic. Its purpose is to make the coupling explicit, not to specify the exact magnitude of the coupling. In practice, the rivalry strengths are populated from the EFC's rival_claims and their associated status vectors.

F.4 The Heavy-Tailed Distribution of Death Times

Let T denote the time from first published refutation to widespread abandonment. Empirical observation across the twelve canonical cases suggests that T follows a heavy-tailed distribution. A natural candidate is the log-normal:

\log T \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu, \sigma^2),

with \mu and \sigma estimated from the data. A preliminary fit to the twelve cases suggests \mu \approx 3.5 (years) and \sigma \approx 1.2.

The heavy tail has a structural explanation. Fast deaths require a clean experimental result with a small belt. Slow deaths occur when the belt is large, when institutional support is present, or when no rival is available. Since these conditions are not rare, slow deaths are not rare.

F.5 The Coherence-Truth Gap

Let \text{Coh}(X) denote a coherence measure on a text X (e.g., internal consistency, absence of contradiction, plausibility). Let \text{Tru}(X) denote a truth measure (agreement with the world). The fundamental observation of §13.1 is that

\text{Coh}(X) \not\Rightarrow \text{Tru}(X).

More precisely, there exist X with \text{Coh}(X) high and \text{Tru}(X) low. The ratio

\rho(X) = \frac{\text{Tru}(X)}{\text{Coh}(X)}

is bounded above by 1 but can be arbitrarily small. LLMs generate X with high \text{Coh} at low cost; the question is what \rho they achieve.

The Atlas's grounding layer is a partial answer: it constrains X to agree with a verified corpus at the level of cited spans. But grounding does not close the gap entirely; the corpus itself may be biased, incomplete, or wrong. The Atlas reduces but does not eliminate the coherence-truth gap.

F.6 Generator–Kritik Cost Asymmetry

Let c_G(t) and c_K(t) be the marginal costs of generating and refuting a claim at time t, measured in some common unit (researcher-hours, computational cost, or currency). In the pre-LLM regime,

c_G(t) \approx c_K(t),

since both required human cognitive effort.

In the LLM regime,

c_G(t) = \epsilon(t), \qquad c_K(t) = \kappa(t),

with \epsilon(t) \ll \kappa(t). The ratio

r(t) = \frac{c_G(t)}{c_K(t)}

diverges as \epsilon(t) \to 0.

The Atlas's adversarial protocol is a design for reducing \kappa(t) without increasing \epsilon(t). The grounding layer is the constraint that prevents \kappa(t) from collapsing to the trivial cost of an unconstrained attack (which would be \epsilon(t) itself, and would be useless).

