Appendix L — Reflexive Methodological Note

This dissertation is a theoretical work. It contains no experimental results, no observational data, and no deployed pipeline. Every claim in it is a claim about how scientific claims might be represented, not a claim about how they are represented in any existing system. The framework has not been validated empirically; its status is open.

The appendices that accompany it should be read in the same spirit. The JSON schema is a proposal, not a standard. The graph ontology is a design, not an implementation. The adversarial protocol is a specification, not a running system. The case studies are library tests, not empirical validations. The failure mode catalogue is a forecast, not a record.

The reader is invited to apply the framework to this dissertation. The claim "the Atlas reduces the adoption rate of obsolete beliefs" has a killer-proof question. It has an auxiliary belt. It has a status vector. It has, as Chapter 9 documents, its own auto-failure card.

The framework does not exempt itself. It would be a poor framework if it did.

